Can i set size limit for snaphot like 20% of disk size like in stoarge snapshot

No, Ceph RBD snapshots do not support a direct snapshot reserve limit like:

Snapshot can grow only up to 20% of volume size

which some SAN/storage arrays provide.

Ceph snapshots are CoW-based and grow dynamically according to changed blocks, with no per-snapshot quota mechanism.

What Ceph Supports Instead
1. Pool Quotas (Closest Alternative)

You can limit total storage usage of a pool.

Example:

ceph osd pool set-quota vm-pool max_bytes 10T

or:

ceph osd pool set-quota vm-pool max_objects 1000000

This limits entire pool usage, including:

VM data
Snapshots
Clones

Check quota:

ceph osd pool get-quota vm-pool
2. Image Size Limit

You already define virtual disk size:

rbd create vm-pool/vm101-disk --size 100G

But snapshots are not separately quota-controlled.

3. Snapshot Retention Automation (Recommended)

Instead of size limits, production environments usually control:

Number of snapshots
Snapshot age
Backup retention

Example policy:

Keep only:
- 3 daily snapshots
- 2 weekly snapshots
- delete older ones automatically

This is the common Ceph approach.

4. Monitor Snapshot Growth

You can monitor usage using:

rbd du vm-pool/vm101-disk

and:

rbd snap ls vm-pool/vm101-disk
5. Advanced Alternative — Separate Snapshot Pool

Some environments isolate workloads:

Pool	Purpose
fast-vm-pool	Active VM disks
backup-pool	Snapshot clones/backups

Then snapshot clones are copied/exported elsewhere.

Important Difference From SAN Storage

Traditional SAN snapshot systems often reserve metadata area:

Snapshot reserve = 20%

If reserve fills:

snapshots fail
or oldest snapshots deleted

Ceph does NOT work like this.

Ceph snapshots consume normal cluster storage dynamically.

Production Recommendation

Instead of snapshot size limits:

Use:
Pool quotas
Monitoring
Snapshot cleanup automation
Backup retention policies
Avoid:
Long-lived snapshots
Hundreds of snapshots
Keeping snapshots on nearly full clusters
Example Cleanup Script

Keep only latest 5 snapshots:

rbd snap ls vm-pool/vm101-disk \
| awk 'NR>1 {print $2}' \
| head -n -5 \
| while read snap; do
    rbd snap rm vm-pool/vm101-disk@$snap
done

You can schedule via cron.

------------------------------------------
Option B — Use QEMU Guest Agent (Best Practice)

Install guest agent inside VM:

Ubuntu/Debian
apt install qemu-guest-agent
systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent
RHEL/CentOS
yum install qemu-guest-agent
systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent

Enable in VM XML:

<channel type='unix'>

Then libvirt can create quiesced snapshots.

4. Create Snapshot Using virsh
External snapshot (recommended)
virsh snapshot-create-as \
--domain vm01 \
--name before-upgrade \
--disk-only \
--atomic

If guest agent is installed:

virsh snapshot-create-as \
--domain vm01 \
--name before-upgrade \
--disk-only \
--quiesce \
--atomic
5. Rollback to Snapshot
Stop VM
virsh shutdown vm01
Rollback
rbd snap rollback rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade
Start VM
virsh start vm01
6. Delete Snapshot
rbd snap rm rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade
7. Important Production Notes
RBD snapshots are copy-on-write, so creation is very fast.
Too many snapshots can reduce performance.
Always test rollback procedure.
For databases like MySQL/PostgreSQL/MongoDB, use application-aware backup if possible.
For long-term backup, export snapshots:
rbd export rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade backup.img
8. Check Existing Snapshots for All Images
rbd ls rbd-pool

Loop through:

for img in $(rbd ls rbd-pool); do
    echo "Image: $img"
    rbd snap ls rbd-pool/$img
done
9. If Using qcow2 on CephFS Instead of RBD

Then snapshot method changes:

virsh snapshot-create-as vm01 snap1

or use:

qemu-img snapshot

But for performance and production environments, direct RBD disks are generally better than qcow2 over CephFS.
